= Related Work <chapter:related-work>

Research on chlorination control in water distribution networks has developed along several lines. Early work treated dosing as a scheduling problem over a known hydraulic and water-quality model. More recent approaches use feedback control, data-driven prediction, or #acr("RL"). A second body of work studies how learned dynamics and expert demonstrations can reduce the interaction required by model-free #acr("RL"). The study at hand connects these lines by using a learned chlorine forecaster and #acr("MPC") only to generate teacher demonstrations. The resulting control behavior is transferred to a recurrent policy that is fine-tuned online.

#cite-author(<boccelli1998booster>) formulated periodic booster-disinfection scheduling as a deterministic linear program that minimizes injected disinfectant mass while enforcing lower and upper residual bounds @boccelli1998booster. The formulation assumes known periodic hydraulics, linear decay kinetics, and fixed booster locations. This established chlorine dosing as a network-wide optimization problem, but produced an open-loop schedule rather than a learned feedback policy. #cite-author(<wang2021waterqualitympc>) later derived a time-varying state-space model for disinfectant transport and developed both an #acr("MPC") formulation and an analytical plug-and-play control law for real-time water-quality regulation @wang2021waterqualitympc. Their approach remains dependent on the water-quality model, state estimates, and hydraulic forecasts, whereas the deployed controller in this study uses none of these components.

Subsequent work has addressed uncertainty and reduced dependence on explicit state-space models. #cite-author(<vrachimis2024delay>) computed robust open-loop dosing schedules for bounded, flow-induced input-delay uncertainty @vrachimis2024delay. Feedback from continuing chlorine measurements is not part of their method and is identified as a future extension. #cite-author(<perelman2025deepc>) instead applied data-enabled predictive control to pressure management and chlorine disinfection using recorded input-output trajectories rather than an identified physical model @perelman2025deepc. Their chlorine case repeatedly optimizes three booster inputs online from measurements at three sensor nodes. In contrast to this online predictive optimization, the present study uses predictive control only to generate offline teacher actions.

The AI for Drinking Water Chlorination Challenge introduced a recent sensor-based benchmark in which controllers regulate several chlorine injectors under unobserved contamination and limited measurements @artelt2025chlorinationchallenge. Its reports include approaches based on #acr("PPO"), as well as rule-based, predictive, and evolutionary approaches. The report by #cite-author(<kowalski2025challenge>) is particularly relevant because it separately investigated #acr("PPO"), a controller combining #acr("LSTM") forecasting with #acr("MPC"), and a rule-based controller within the same challenge @kowalski2025challenge. These alternatives were not combined into one pipeline and were only partially evaluated. Their #acr("MPC") setup used the learned predictor to optimize action sequences online at every control step, but the computational cost prevented the evaluation of complete #acr("MPC") episodes. In the same challenge, #cite-author(<abzanov2025chlorine>) investigated #acr("PPO") variants including an #acr("LSTM")-based recurrent policy @abzanov2025chlorine. The challenge also differs from the single-source Hanoi problem studied here through its multiple injectors and its sensor and contamination setup. The methodological connection therefore concerns the use of a forecaster, #acr("MPC"), and #acr("PPO") rather than the complete control problem. The present study restricts predictive optimization to offline teacher generation and evaluates whether the resulting actions can be transferred to a recurrent policy through pretraining, so neither the forecaster nor the optimizer is required afterward.

#cite-author(<monsia2026neuroevolution>) used an #acr("LSTM") surrogate to support multi-objective neuroevolution of neural chlorination controllers, updating the surrogate with trajectories from simulator evaluations @monsia2026neuroevolution. Their approach places the learned dynamics within the evolutionary controller search, whereas the present study uses them to generate #acr("MPC") teacher actions.

Outside the domain of water distribution networks and water-quality control, model-based #acr("RL") provides established precedents for combining learned dynamics with online planning. The #acr("PETS") algorithm uses probabilistic ensemble models, trajectory sampling, and the #acr("CEM") to select actions while accounting for model uncertainty @chua2018pets. This supports the use of sample-based optimization over a learned dynamics model, although the forecaster in the present study is deterministic. #cite-author(<nagabandi2018dynamics>) took a related approach. They trained neural dynamics models, generated trajectories with #acr("MPC"), cloned the #acr("MPC") policy, and then fine-tuned it with model-free #acr("RL") @nagabandi2018dynamics. Their experiments concern fully observed locomotion and iteratively query the #acr("MPC") teacher through #acr("DAgger"). The present study examines the same general transfer from predictive control to model-free learning in a partially observable chlorination task, using a fixed cross-fitted teacher dataset, a recurrent actor, and RecurrentPPO fine-tuning.

Work on #acr("MPC") imitation clarifies the trade-off introduced by a fixed dataset. #cite-author(<ahn2023mpcimitation>) analyze imitation of constrained linear #acr("MPC") and propose an on-policy procedure because ordinary behavior cloning may encounter states outside the teacher distribution @ahn2023mpcimitation. #acr("DAgger") addresses the same sequential distri-?bution shift by repeatedly collecting learner trajectories and requesting expert labels for the visited states @ross2011dagger. These procedures reduce mismatch between training and deployment states, but require continued access to the expert. The pipeline studied here deliberately separates teacher generation from policy learning. After offline teacher generation, subsequent #acr("PPO") fine-tuning allows the policy to adapt through reward feedback without further #acr("MPC") queries or expert annotation.

#cite-author(<nair2018demonstrations>) combined off-policy #acr("RL") with demonstration replay and an auxiliary behavior-cloning loss to support exploration in sparse-reward manipulation tasks @nair2018demonstrations. Their method continues to use demonstrations during #acr("RL"), whereas demonstration supervision in the present study ends before online learning begins.

Prior work therefore covers the main components of the proposed pipeline, including chlorine optimization, learned predictive control, #acr("MPC") imitation, and #acr("RL") from demonstrations. The experiment in this thesis focuses on their combination under partial observability and asks whether cross-fitted offline #acr("MPC") supervision improves RecurrentPPO under the same online interaction budget as training from an ordinary initialization.
